At any concrete point in any concrete game we have the possibility to
choose from the options you accumulated and the game actually presents.
Some options may clearly save us from the death, some will not, and in
some cases we don't even know which move or option use might save us.

Now consider a fixed (not Nethack), but very complex (with many variable
parameters like Nethack) problem domain. We can find a solution domain
that typically has local optima, but generally no absolute optimum. Local
optima and given global optima may or may not exceed the necessary value
of threshhold to solve your problem (in Nethack global solution would be
to survive and leave the dungeon with The Amulet); but you can at least
calculate the global optimum and operate perfectly (in theory at least,
depending on the number of variables to consider; it can as well be a NP
complete poroblem, practically impossible to calculate or estimate).

In Nethack we have no fixed rather a dynamic and complex problem domain,
depending on random events in many respects. There's no global a priori
optimal solution one can calculate. (A multi dimensional wave function
that collapses partly with every move done.) So we have to rely on event
probabilities to select "local" optimal choices from the possibilities
given; we strive to local optima with global goals in mind to overcome
the worst suboptimal local maxima. Typically players know probabilities
or have at least some feeling from their own experience. In Nethack we
may make strategic plans depending on knowledge how the game "works" and
based on experience made to determine which move to make, which milestones
and goals to focus. Any fifty-fifty chance, for example, in a concrete
case may lead to a local optimum with both decisions, or neither decision
will solve the problem, depending on the randomness of events.

If we feel the need to define the term "avoidable deaths" we should be
aware of that.

